import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Your first mission","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/first-mission.md","filePath":"guide/first-mission.md"}'),a={name:"guide/first-mission.md"};function n(r,e,h,l,c,d){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="your-first-mission" tabindex="-1">Your first mission <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-first-mission" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your first mission&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The first time you open the console after installing Orbit, Ted has a mission ready. It opens with a one-minute briefing in six short chapters and then lists the steps that turn a fresh install into a cloud you can trust. You can skip anything, close it at any point and pick it up later from the card on the overview.</p><h2 id="briefing" tabindex="-1">The briefing <a class="header-anchor" href="#briefing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The briefing {#briefing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Six chapters, each a short scene that plays on its own:</p><ol><li><strong>Cloud.</strong> Your servers become one cloud, and with three of them, losing one leaves apps, databases and Orbit itself running on the others.</li><li><strong>Ship.</strong> A GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket repository, an image or a <a href="./catalog">catalog</a> app goes live with an HTTPS address.</li><li><strong>Data.</strong> Backups on a schedule, copies off the server and restore tests, plus <a href="./spaces">Spaces</a> for files.</li><li><strong>Network.</strong> <a href="./dns">DNS</a> at Cloudflare, DigitalOcean or Hostinger, <a href="./tunnel">Cloudflare Tunnel</a>, <a href="./network-exits">network exits</a> and the <a href="./uptime">status page</a>.</li><li><strong>Yours.</strong> <a href="./themes">Themes</a>, <a href="./members">members</a>, <a href="./api-tokens">API tokens</a>, the <a href="./wiki">wiki</a> and the terminal.</li><li><strong>Ted.</strong> Warnings before something breaks, incidents explained with evidence, what is new after each update and <a href="./ai-machines">AI machines</a>.</li></ol><p>A bar under the scene fills while a chapter plays, and the next one starts when it is full. Reading the text or moving the keyboard focus into the briefing holds it; choosing a chapter, the arrow keys, <strong>Back</strong> or <strong>Next chapter</strong> hand the pace over to you. <strong>Skip the briefing</strong> goes straight to the steps. With reduced motion, or with animations paused in Settings, every scene shows its final picture and nothing advances by itself.</p><h2 id="steps" tabindex="-1">The steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The steps {#steps}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each step says in one line why it matters and can be settled in three ways: done right there, done somewhere else in the console and checked automatically, or skipped for now.</p><ol><li><strong>Secure your account.</strong> Set up the second factor and save the recovery codes, in the mission itself.</li><li><strong>Set Orbit&#39;s clock.</strong> Choose the <a href="./time-zone">time zone</a> schedules, notifications and the status page follow.</li><li><strong>Choose where alerts go.</strong> Add an email, Slack or Discord <a href="./notifications">channel</a> without leaving the mission.</li><li><strong>Ship your first project.</strong> Opens the project creation; the step checks itself once an application has deployed.</li><li><strong>Give it your domain.</strong> Opens the <a href="./dns">DNS page</a>; the step checks itself once a custom domain is verified.</li><li><strong>Keep your data safe.</strong> Opens Backups to add an S3 storage; the step checks itself once one is ready.</li><li><strong>Add a second server.</strong> Opens the server setup; the step checks itself once a second server is ready.</li></ol><p>Members invited to a workspace get the first two steps only. Steps already set up before the mission opens are checked on their own, so nobody is asked twice.</p><h2 id="coming-back" tabindex="-1">Coming back <a class="header-anchor" href="#coming-back" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coming back {#coming-back}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The mission is kept with your account, so it follows you to any browser. Until it is finished, the overview shows <strong>Mission control</strong> with the next step. Afterwards, <strong>Settings → General → The mission handbook</strong> replays the briefing and shows the mission again; the command palette has <strong>Replay the onboarding</strong> too. Replaying never undoes anything you set up.</p><h2 id="report" tabindex="-1">For administrators <a class="header-anchor" href="#report" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;For administrators {#report}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The handbook card also shows how far the people using this Orbit got: how many started, how many finished, and which steps were done or skipped. The numbers come from the users&#39; own preferences on this server; nothing is sent anywhere.</p>',14)])])}const f=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,f as default};
